{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"80d1785d-ba55-40a4-86cf-2016edcabc8e","title":"Projectile motion: horizontal and vertical velocity"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"80d1785d-ba55-40a4-86cf-2016edcabc8e","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Ignoring air resistance, a projectile follows a curved path. Its velocity splits into independent horizontal and vertical components.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Projectile velocity"},{"op":"figure","id":"flight","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-0.5,11.5],"y":[-1,7],"axes":"edge","equal_scale":true,"x_label":"x (m)","y_label":"y (m)"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"path","y":"2*x-0.2*x^2","domain":[0,10]},{"kind":"point","id":"launch","at":{"on":"path","x":0},"label":"launch"},{"kind":"point","id":"rising","at":{"on":"path","x":2.5}},{"kind":"point","id":"top","at":{"on":"path","x":5}},{"kind":"point","id":"falling","at":{"on":"path","x":7.5}}]},{"op":"equation","id":"initial","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"v_{0x}=5\\,\\mathrm{m/s},\\quad v_{0y}=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"80d1785d-ba55-40a4-86cf-2016edcabc8e","step":{"id":"s2","script":"No horizontal force means no horizontal acceleration. These equal arrows show the same horizontal velocity throughout the flight.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"horizontal","on":"flight","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"rising"},"to":[3.5,3.75],"arrow":true,"label":"5 m/s"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"top"},"to":[6,5],"arrow":true,"label":"5 m/s"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"falling"},"to":[8.5,3.75],"arrow":true,"label":"5 m/s"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"vx","anchor":{"el":"initial","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"a_x=0\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad v_x=5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"80d1785d-ba55-40a4-86cf-2016edcabc8e","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Gravity changes only vertical velocity. Using gravity as ten meters per second squared, upward velocity decreases to zero, then becomes downward.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"vertical","on":"flight","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"rising"},"to":[2.5,4.75],"arrow":true,"label":"+5 m/s"},{"kind":"text","at":[5,5.8],"text":"vertical: 0"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"falling"},"to":[7.5,2.75],"arrow":true,"label":"−5 m/s"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"vy","anchor":{"el":"vx","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"a_y=-g,\\quad g\\approx10\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\qquad v_y=10-10t"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"At the highest point, is the projectile's total velocity zero?","expected_gist":"No. Vertical velocity is zero, but horizontal velocity remains 5 m/s."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"80d1785d-ba55-40a4-86cf-2016edcabc8e","step":{"id":"s4","script":"At the peak, motion is entirely horizontal. Elsewhere, combining both components gives a velocity tangent to the path.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"resultants","on":"flight","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"rising"},"to":[3.5,4.75],"arrow":true,"label":"v"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"falling"},"to":[8.5,2.75],"arrow":true,"label":"v"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"peak","anchor":{"el":"vy","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"t=1\\,\\mathrm{s}:\\quad v_y=0,\\quad |\\vec v|=v_x=5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
